.TH EUCLID-CLI-ENS-GET-MESSAGE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ens-get-message \- show one message, by its id
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ens get-message
.BR \-\-message\-id | \-m
.I ID
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints one message as JSON: its body, its attributes, which topic
it belongs to, its status and the timestamps that go with it.
.PP
By message id, not by receipt handle. A receipt handle belongs to one
delivery and is void once that delivery's claim has expired, while the
id names the message for as long as it exists \- and asking about a
message is something one does after the fact, which is exactly when a
receipt handle is no longer any use.
.PP
This is the same description
.BR euclid-cli-ens-list-messages (1)
gives of each of its own, for a single message. They share one
representation on purpose, so that a field added to a listing is in this
too rather than the two drifting apart.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-message\-id " " \fIID\fR
The message id, as
.BR euclid-cli-ens-publish-message (1)
returned it and
.BR euclid-cli-ens-list-messages (1)
shows it. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The message is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, or no such
message exists.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ens get-message --message-id 7f0c8a1e-2b44-4a0f-9d61-1c2f3e4a5b60
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ens (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-list-messages (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-get-topic (1)
